# Mueller's Lorentz-slope paper mapped onto "Gravity Across Scales"

Source: A. Mueller, "The Lorentz Factor as the Slope of an Additional Spatial Coordinate: Embedding, Painlevé–Gullstrand Flow, and the Issue Of Simultaneity" (Zenodo v1, 16 Nov 2026). Mapped to: P. N. Grossi, *Gravity Across Scales: Nano, Meso, and Macro Instantiations of the dm3 Operator Chain* (Principia Orthogona series, 2026).

Status: prepared 20 Nov 2026, same format as `FERREIRA_REVIEW_MAP.md`. It uses the referee ledger in `../referee/mueller-lorentz-slope/LEDGER.md` for what was and was not read in Mueller's paper (not read: §1, §2.1, §2.3.6, §2.4.1–2.4.2, §4.1–4.5, Appendix A.1–A.3, A.6, Appendix B derivations). Nothing is quoted. Rows that say the paper "should" do something are editorial suggestions for the author, not claims about the physics. Nothing here is sent to anyone.

## What the two papers share and where they differ

Both place physics on a geometric object with an extra direction: Mueller embeds space as a hypersurface in a flat four-dimensional space whose fourth coordinate has slope equal to the Lorentz factor's sinh-part; the dm3 paper uses a contact 3-manifold with Reeb field ∂z. They are different constructions. Mueller's has a metric, imported field equations, and checkable numbers; the dm3 paper has an ODE and no metric, and labels its physical claims conjectured. Mueller's algebra checks (24 Lean theorems, sympy, numbers), which says nothing about whether the dm3 conjectures hold.

## Mapping

| Mueller | Paper section | Relation | Suggested action |
|---|---|---|---|
| Extra coordinate y with ∂ₓy = sinh of rapidity, γ = √(1+(∂ₓy)²) | 6 (Hopf fibre, M-theory circle), T4 (Reeb field ∂z) | Both add a direction. In Mueller's it is a spacelike embedding coordinate, flat and non-compact; in the dm3 paper it is the Reeb direction of a contact form. No map between them is given by either. | Do not identify the Reeb field with Mueller's fourth coordinate without writing down a map. |
| Metric, Hamiltonian constraint imported from GR; Proposition 1 (lapse √(1−rₛ/R)) | 2, 12 | Mueller states field equations and says what is imported. The dm3 chain has no spacetime metric. | Same point as the Ferreira and Hawking–Ellis rows: say the dm3 chain is a dynamical-systems model, and place it against Mueller-style explicit equations only once it has some. |
| Circular orbits: L² = GMR²/(R−3GM/c²) (checked in sympy) | 3 (T1), 4 (T2, fold r*), 9 (Conj. 9.1) | The expression has a pole at R = 3GM/c² and its minimum at R = 6GM/c² (my calculation from the quoted formula: setting d/dR of R²/(R−3M) to zero). That minimum is the standard innermost stable orbit, a fold in the radial dynamics. | Same suggestion as the Hawking–Ellis row: compare r* with the 6M fold stating where the two differ; use Mueller's formula only as a pointer to a standard result. |
| Hubble flow β_H² = 2Gm/(c²R), critical density L = c/H, Sciama identity (checked; follows from the critical-density definition) | 9 (Conj. 9.1 Hill sphere, Milkomeda), 11.4 | Mueller connects a local escape-velocity construction to the Hubble flow. The dm3 paper's galactic claim is a Hill-sphere fold with no radius derived. | For Open Problem 11.4, a candidate observable is where the Hubble-flow term overtakes local binding; this is a suggestion, not a derivation. |
| Hypothesis M: g₀ = κ′cH₀, κ′ = 1/2π as a candidate; fit with free scale gives 0.35 | T* = 2π (self-dual radius), 7 | The factor 2π appears in both. In Mueller's it is a candidate normalisation, factor-two off his own fit. In the dm3 paper it is the period of the limit cycle. | Do not connect the two 2π's. Neither paper derives the other's value. |
| Appendix A numerical alignments (kinematic velocity vs CMB dipole, one-parameter fit by the paper's own account) | 12 Empirical grounding | The dm3 paper's list consists of independent measurements anchoring statements, not fits. Mueller's alignments are one-parameter fits. | If cited, say they are fits, not tests. |
| Cassini bound ω_BD ≳ 4×10⁴ applied to G44; G44 = 1 follows from how y is defined (ledger finding 1) | 12 | An example of a stated empirical constraint on an extra-coordinate construction. | Any dm3 field-theory version should say what it predicts for the analogous quantity. |
| Equivalence to GR claimed only for vacuum or pressureless spherical cases | 1 (lock gap sentence) | Mueller's own claim stops at reproducing GR where GR is already tested. The dm3 sentence that GR "does not explain" the lock gap goes further. | Keep the earlier suggestion: state that the lock-gap claim is a re-description, not a deviation, unless a differing prediction is identified. |

## Overall

Mueller's paper shows what the dm3 gravity claims would look like with explicit equations, imported assumptions listed, and numbers recomputable. It does not support or contradict T1–T4, and I see no derivation linking the two. The useful transfer is stylistic: a ledger of checked, imported and assumed items, which the dm3 paper could adopt for Sections 9–12.
